---
/**
 * The one page on this site that computes rather than asserts.
 *
 * The ranking it prints is right for one bracket and wrong for another, which is why it
 * may not be printed as a table of constants. Every figure here is `~/lib/placement`
 * arithmetic over `~/content/placement` data, run at whatever rates the reader gives it.
 *
 * The worked example below is rendered on the server from the same functions the island
 * runs, so a reader with scripting switched off still gets the answer and the queue.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import PlacementTool from "~/components/islands/PlacementTool";
import { fill, forfeitedCreditBp, percentOfBp, queue, ratePercent, shelf } from "~/components/islands/placement-model";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { defaultTaxRegime, placementAsOf, priorityRule, rothVersusTraditionalNote } from "~/content/placement";
import { derivedRates } from "~/lib/placement";

/** Equal thirds in a Roth, a traditional account and a taxable one. */
const SHELTER_SHARE = 0.6667;

const opening = queue(shelf("paid-out"), defaultTaxRegime);
const placed = fill(opening, SHELTER_SHARE);
const forfeited = forfeitedCreditBp(placed);
const rates = derivedRates(defaultTaxRegime);

const foreign = opening.filter((row) => row.international);
const domestic = opening.filter((row) => row.international === false);
const weakestForeign = foreign[foreign.length - 1];
const strongestDomestic = domestic[0];
const last = opening[opening.length - 1];

const WHERE = { shelter: "Roth or traditional", split: "Partly sheltered", taxable: "Taxable" } as const;

/**
 * Written once, read twice: into `<thead>`, and into the label each cell shows when
 * the table becomes a list of cards below 40rem.
 */
const COLUMNS = ["#", "Fund", "Share", "Saved per dollar sheltered", "Goes in"] as const;
---

<Base
  title="Which account should each fund go in?"
  description="A calculator for the seven funds in the most ambitious portfolio here. Tell it your tax bracket and how your money is split between account types, and it says which fund goes in a Roth, a traditional account, or a taxable account, and why."
>
  <Hero
    title="Which account should each fund go in?"
    lede="The same seven funds cost you different amounts of tax depending on which account holds them. This calculator ranks them."
  />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What it answers</h2>

    <p>You have some money in a Roth, some in a traditional IRA or 401(k), and some in an ordinary taxable
    brokerage account. The funds that throw off the most taxable income each year belong in the sheltered
    accounts; the ones that throw off the least belong in the taxable account, where the shelter would be wasted
    on them. The calculator works out that ranking for the seven funds in
    <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">the portfolio with trend</a>: RSST, VTI, VTV, VXUS, AVDV, IDMO and AVES.
    For each one it says where it goes and why in one line.</p>

    <p>The short version is stable across every tax bracket I ran: every international fund goes into a
    sheltered account before any US fund, RSST and IDMO go first, and VTI goes last, so it is the fund that lives
    in the taxable account.</p>

    <h2>What it needs from you</h2>

    <p>Two things. Your federal tax bracket, or your own rates if you know them, and roughly what share of your
    investments sits in each type of account. It does not ask your income, your balance, or anything that
    identifies you, and nothing you type leaves your browser. The button underneath writes your setup into the
    address bar, if you want to send it to someone.</p>
  </div>

<Breakout class="mt-6">
    <PlacementTool client:visible />
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-12">
    <h2>Why foreign funds go in first</h2>

    <p>You have probably read the opposite: hold international funds in the taxable account so you can claim
    the foreign tax credit. Sheltering all four of them here does give up
    <span data-numeric>{percentOfBp(forfeited)}</span> a year of credit, permanently. It saves several times
    that, because foreign funds pay bigger dividends, and a bigger dividend in a taxable account is a bigger tax
    bill. At <span data-numeric>{ratePercent(rates.qualifiedDividend)}%</span> on qualified dividends, the
    weakest foreign case is {weakestForeign?.ticker} at
    <span data-numeric>{percentOfBp(weakestForeign?.priorityBp ?? 0)}</span> saved a year per dollar sheltered,
    against {strongestDomestic?.ticker} at
    <span data-numeric>{percentOfBp(strongestDomestic?.priorityBp ?? 0)}</span> for the strongest US case.</p>

    <p>{last?.ticker} comes last, at <span data-numeric>{percentOfBp(last?.priorityBp ?? 0)}</span>. It is the
    cheapest, broadest, calmest fund on the list, which is exactly why it belongs in the account with no
    protection.</p>

    <p>{rothVersusTraditionalNote}</p>

    <h2>What it does not know</h2>

    <p>State tax, which it ignores entirely. The menu of funds your employer&rsquo;s plan actually offers, which
    may not include most of these. Gains already built up in a taxable account, which can make moving a fund
    cost more than the shelter saves. And how RSST&rsquo;s income will be taxed, which its own filings leave
    open; the calculator lets you choose either reading, and the ranking barely changes.</p>

    <h2>What it is worth</h2>

    <p>Against a sensible default, putting each fund in its best account saves about 2 to 7 hundredths of a
    percent a year. It is worth considerably more if you would otherwise have held the international funds in a
    taxable account, and it is worth nothing if all your money is in one type of account.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <h2>The queue at equal thirds</h2>

    <p>One investor, equal thirds in a Roth, a traditional account and a taxable brokerage account, at the top
    federal rates, counting only what RSST has paid out. Rendered here whether or not the tool above loaded.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout scroll class="mt-6" label="The seven funds, ranked by what a sheltered dollar saves" maxWidth="42rem">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base sm:min-w-[36rem]">
      <caption class="sr-only">
        The seven funds ranked by what a sheltered dollar of each saves a year, at the top marginal rates, with
        two thirds of the portfolio sheltered
      </caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          {
            COLUMNS.map((heading, i) => (
              <th
                role="columnheader"
                scope="col"
                class:list={["eyebrow py-2", i === 0 ? "pr-3 text-right" : "pr-4", (i === 2 || i === 3) && "text-right"]}
              >
                {heading}
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-muted">
        {
          placed.map((row, index) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule last:border-0">
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[0]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-3 text-right text-ink-faint">
                {index + 1}
              </td>
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal text-ink">
                {row.ticker}
              </th>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[2]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">
                {ratePercent(row.weight, 1)}%
              </td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[3]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-semibold text-ink">
                {percentOfBp(row.priorityBp)}
              </td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[4]} class="py-2 pr-4">
                {WHERE[row.where]}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-12">
    <h2>Three things this rests on</h2>

    <p><strong>How much of each dividend gets the low rate.</strong> Part of what a fund pays out is taxed at
    the dividend rate and the rest at your income rate, which is 17 points dearer at the top bracket. Where a
    fund files that split, the tool uses the filed figure: <span data-numeric>44.48%</span> for AVES,
    <span data-numeric>25%</span> for IDMO. For VXUS and AVDV it borrows the figures Vanguard filed for the
    funds they most resemble, and for VTI and VTV it assumes the whole dividend gets the low rate. Each
    assumption pushes its fund toward the taxable account, so the plan errs on the cautious side.</p>

    <p><strong>RSST files two readings of itself.</strong> It records income inside itself that it has not
    paid out. Count only what shareholders have been taxed on and it sits near the bottom of the queue; count
    everything and it goes straight to the top. It belongs in a sheltered account either way, and the cost of
    getting this wrong is about ten times larger in one direction than the other, so the tool shelters it.</p>

    <p><strong>Your yields are not these yields.</strong> Every figure is US federal, on filings as of
    <span data-numeric>{placementAsOf}</span>. State income tax is left out and adds to every line. A yield is
    the input a ranking like this is most sensitive to, and none of these is guaranteed to hold next year.</p>

    <Callout kind="caveat">
      <p>Nothing here is personal advice. Every figure is a function of stated inputs, and a different
      investor should restate them.</p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Base>
